When a small group of living creatures (a small population) moves away from the main one they come (e.g. if they move into a mountainous chain or into a river, or if they move to a new island so that they can't regress easily) they'll often find themselves in a different environment than they used to be accustomed to.
The new environment boasts different resources and competitors and therefore a new population will need different characteristics or adaptations from those it had before to be a strong competitor.
There have been no changes in the original population, which still require the same adaptations as in the past.
As time passes, as the incipient population adapts to the new environment, it begins to resemble less and less to the other population.
Ultimately, after thousands or even, millions of years, the two populations will look so different that it will not be possible to classify them as from the same species.
This process is called speciation, which simply means the development of new species, and is an inescapable consequence and a fundamental part of evolution.
