Technological determinism is a concept that grazes, pragmatically, a wide range of ideas, from technological impulse or impulse to a strict sense that there is an underlying logic that impels the destiny of humanity and that is related to the laws of science and how they are reflected in technology.
Almost all the interpretations of technological determinism have in common two general ideas: that technological development itself unfolds on a path independent of any cultural or political influence and that, at the same time, technology has "effects" in societies that are inherent and not socially conditioned.
To give an example, one could argue that the existence of cars necessarily results in the development of roads.
However, a national network of roads is not economically viable for a reduced volume of cars, thus designing new manufacturing techniques to reduce the costs of owning a vehicle.
The massive use of cars also generates a greater incidence in traffic accidents, which leads to the development of new techniques in the health field to repair damaged bodies.
